Source header “Scaling horizontal-tube logarithmic rates”; Public declarations: Let $
u$ be a probability law, $\sigma>0$, and let $(x_n)$ and $(t_n)$ be arbitrary real and natural-number sequences. If the standardized law $
u_\sigma=
u\circ(x\mapsto x/\sigma)^{-1}$ satisfies \[  rac{(x_n/\sigma)^2}{t_n} \log\!\left(\mathbb P_{
u_\sigma} \{S_j\in[-a x_n/\sigma,(1-a)x_n/\sigma]:1\le j\le t_n\}ight) \longrightarrow C, \] then \[  rac{x_n^2}{t_n} \log\!\left(\mathbb P_{
u} \{S_j\in[-a x_n,(1-a)x_n]:1\le j\le t_n\}ight) \longrightarrow \sigma^2 C. \] This follows from `horizontalTubeProbability_map_div`; no moment or scale assumption is required. The public theorem is `ProbabilityTheory.RandomWalk.SmallDeviation.HorizontalTube.tendsto_scaledLogProbability_of_map_div`.
